Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

hi all

I had what I thought was a reasonably straightforward application of adply. I had a large, very sparse data frame, where I need a variable indicating how many NA cells were in each row. The following is an indicative example

library(plyr)
set.seed(0)
df <- data.frame(matrix(rep(sample(c(LETTERS[1:3],NA),size=30, replace=T),times = 200000),
                        nrow = 200000))

system.time(adply(df, 1, function(i) sum(is.na <http://is.na>(i)), .progress = "text"))
>   user  system elapsed
> 557.98   68.21  629.01

My PC is old, no question (quad core, 2.4ghz, 4gb ram) but I'm still surprised that it took 10 minutes to do this operation.

I'm guessing my approach is inefficient--what's a better way to do this?

Thanks in advance

Tom

From u0c39e60ddb38622b at groups.google.com  Wed Jun 20 23:06:38 2012
